### Effect of various parameters on monolinuron photocatalytic degradation

Initial herbicide, TiO\({}_{{}_{2}}\) concentrations and photonic flux are important parameters which can affect photocatalytic degradation rates. The effect of these three parameters has been studied at natural pH (pH = 5), on the photocatalytic degradation of monolinuron (MLN) chosen as the pesticide. For all experiment sets, the data found for the degradation of MLN are in good agreement with a pseudo-first-order reaction with respect to concentrations in the experimental range (0.98 < r\({}^{2}\) <0.99 ).

\[\tau_{\rm MLN}=-\frac{\rm d[\,MLN]}{\rm dt}=\rm k_{\rm obs}[\,MLN] \tag{1}\]

#### 3.3.1 Effect of TiO\({}_{{}_{2}}\) concentration

Figure 7 shows the influence of TiO\({}_{{}_{2}}\) concentration ([TiO\({}_{{}_{2}}\)] = 0.1 to 1.5 g L\({}^{\rm-1}\)) on the degradation rate of MLN at 4 mg L\({}^{\rm-1}\) and for a photonic flux of 250 W m\({}^{\rm-2}\). As expected, the apparent rate constant (k\({}_{\rm obs}\)) was found to increase with the increasing concentration of semiconductor. This enhancement is due to the increase of the number of active sites with the TiO\({}_{{}_{2}}\) concentration. However, k\({}_{\rm obs}\) tends to a limit that corresponds to the total absorption of the photonic flux by the catalyst as illustrated in Figure 7 (average absorbance between 290 and 400 nm of the TiO\({}_{{}_{2}}\) suspension for 4 cm path length versus k\({}_{\rm obs}\)). For our experimental conditions, this limit seems to be reached at 1 g L\({}^{\rm-1}\) for which 99% of the light is absorbed. Above this concentration, a plateau is observed as it is mentioned by several authors (INEL and OKTE, 1996; Le CAMPION _et al._, 1999; PARRA _et al._, 2002; WONG and CHU, 2003). Figure 7 also points out that at low TiO\({}_{{}_{2}}\) concentration (< 0.1 g L\({}^{\rm-1}\)) or weak absorption, the degradation of monolinuron occurs at very low rate.

#### 3.3.2 Effect of herbicide concentration

The effect of the monolinuron concentration has been studied in a range between 0.8 to 8 mg L\({}^{\rm-1}\) for a constant TiO\({}_{{}_{2}}\) dosage ([TiO\({}_{{}_{2}}\)] = 0.2 g L\({}^{\rm-1}\)) and a constant photonic flux (P = 250 W m\({}^{\rm-2}\)). Figure 8 shows that the apparent first-order rate constant k\({}_{\rm obs}\) decreases with the increasing of the initial concentration of MLN. This can be explained by the competition of photoproducts with substrate for the adsorption on the active sites of the TiO\({}_{{}_{2}}\) surface (OLLIS _et al._, 1989; PRAMAURO and VINCENTI, 1993).

In the last few decades, the Langmuir-Hinshelwood expression has been successfully used to describe the dependence of the reaction rate \(\tau_{\rm MLN}\) on the initial concentration of the

